The '''2004–05 Serie A''' (known as the [[Serie A]] [[Telecom Italia Mobile|TIM]] for sponsorship reasons) was the 103rd season of top-tier Italian [[association football|football]], the 73rd in a [[round-robin tournament]]. It was expanded to contain 20 clubs, which played 38 matches against each other, rather than the 34 matches in previous seasons, while relegations were reduced to three. The [[Coppa Campioni d'Italia]] was presented to the winners on the pitch for the first time.

The first two teams qualified directly to [[UEFA Champions League]], teams ending in the third and fourth places had to play Champions League qualifications, teams ending in the fifth and sixth places qualified to [[UEFA Europa League|UEFA Cup]] (another spot was given to the winner of [[Coppa Italia]]), while only the last three teams were to be relegated in [[Serie B]], the Italian second division, following a regulations change.

[[Juventus FC|Juventus]] finished in first place but were later stripped of the title as a result of the [[Calciopoli]] scandal. As a result, there was no winner or runner-up in the 2004-05 competition, but the European competition places for higher positions in the table remained. [[AC Milan|Milan]] ended the campaign in second place, however they are not considered runners-up due to the scandal. [[Udinese Calcio|Udinese]] qualified for the UEFA Champions League for the first time in its history. [[US Città di Palermo|Palermo]], in its first Serie A campaign in over 30 years, finished in sixth place, qualifying for the [[UEFA Cup]] for the first time in its history. [[AS Roma|Roma]] qualified for the UEFA Cup as the runners-up in the [[Coppa Italia]] because the cup winner, [[Inter Milan|Internazionale]], had already qualified for the [[UEFA Champions League|Champions League]].

Two teams, [[Brescia Calcio|Brescia]] and [[Atalanta BC|Atalanta]], were directly relegated to [[Serie B]], while the third relegation place was to be decided among three teams ([[ACF Fiorentina|Fiorentina]], [[Bologna FC 1909|Bologna]] and [[Parma FC|Parma]]), counting only the so-called ''classifica avulsa''; that is, the table composed solely by the six matches among the three teams. Bologna and Parma had fewer points, and played the relegation tiebreaker. The tiebreaker was won by Parma, who were defeated 0–1 at home but won 0–2 away in the return match. This method of classifying teams on equal points totals was abolished for the [[2005–06 Serie A|2005–06 season]] but returned for the [[2022–23 Serie A|2022–23 season]].

